From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 03:34:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: brcmfmac: Nap after 20 ms on the BCM4378 and
 BCM4388

With power save on and the 2000 ms sleep return, the radio stays awake
for two seconds after each frame, so any steady background traffic
keeps it awake almost all the time.

Sweep on a J604 (M4 MacBook Pro 14", BCM4388), power save on, Wi-Fi
rail median of 20 reads, 20 gateway pings per value, no ping lost at
any value:

  pm2_sleep_ret   Wi-Fi rail   gateway p50
  10 ms           101.0 mW     15.8 ms
  20 ms           102.0 mW     15.4 ms
  50 ms           106.5 mW     15.4 ms
  100 ms          123.0 mW     14.8 ms
  200 ms          151.5 mW     14.6 ms
  2000 ms         357.5 mW      5.82 ms

The rail is flat from 10 to 50 ms and climbs from 100 ms up, and 20 ms
is in the flat part. The ping cost shows up as soon as the return drops
below 2000 ms: p50 is 14.6 to 15.8 ms at every value from 200 ms down.

On an M1 MacBook Air (J313, BCM4378), 12 balanced rounds of 30 s on
5 GHz put 20 ms 329 mW (28.5%) below 2000 ms, for +4.3 ms average
gateway RTT, and 200 ms 170 mW (14.7%) below. On two BCM4388 Airs on a
very quiet network, 2000 -> 20 ms saved 33-55 mW (J615) and 53-107 mW
(J713) of total power, with no ping lost.

20 ms is not a firmware value. On the J604 and the J313 the firmware
boots with power save off and a 200 ms return, before the driver
writes one. Skipping the write when timeout < 0 would keep that 200 ms,
which is above the flat part of the curve on both chips.

Nobody has measured a BCM4387 yet, so it keeps 2000 ms.

Use 20 ms as the BCM4378 and BCM4388 default when nobody set a timeout.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 .../broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c         | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c
index fdd00e08206..5ed764bec86 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmfmac/cfg80211.c
@@ -91,6 +91,17 @@
 #define BRCMF_ND_INFO_TIMEOUT		msecs_to_jiffies(2000)
 
 #define BRCMF_PS_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS		2000
+/*
+ * PM2 sleep return used when nobody set a timeout, on the chips where it
+ * was measured. With power save on, a BCM4388 (J604) drew 101, 102 and
+ * 106.5 mW on the Wi-Fi rail at 10, 20 and 50 ms, then 123, 151.5 and
+ * 357.5 mW at 100, 200 and 2000 ms, with no ping lost at any value. On a
+ * BCM4378 (J313), 20 ms drew 329 mW less than 2000 ms for +4.3 ms average
+ * RTT. 20 ms is in the flat part of that curve. It is not read from the
+ * firmware: both chips boot with a 200 ms return, which is above the flat
+ * part. Nobody has measured a BCM4387, so it keeps the 2000 ms maximum.
+ */
+#define BRCMF_PS_NAP_TIMEOUT_MS		20
 
 #define BRCMF_LPAS_UNICAST_FILTER_ID	100
 #define BRCMF_LPAS_MAGIC_FILTER_ID	101
@@ -3484,6 +3495,9 @@ brcmf_cfg80211_dump_station(struct wiphy *wiphy, struct wireless_dev *wdev,
 static u32 brcmf_ps_default_timeout(struct brcmf_pub *drvr)
 {
 	switch (drvr->bus_if->chip) {
+	case BRCM_CC_4378_CHIP_ID:
+	case BRCM_CC_4388_CHIP_ID:
+		return BRCMF_PS_NAP_TIMEOUT_MS;
 	default:
 		return BRCMF_PS_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS;
 	}
-- 
